WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.343 --> 00:00:03.093
(dramatic music)

00:00:07.234 --> 00:00:10.403
(waves crashing)

00:00:10.403 --> 00:00:12.485
- [Narrator] When you
think of the Great Lakes,

00:00:12.485 --> 00:00:14.485
you may not expect this.

00:00:16.388 --> 00:00:19.767
These photographs of
magnificent towering waves

00:00:19.767 --> 00:00:23.222
weren't taken in the
Pacific or the Atlantic.

00:00:23.222 --> 00:00:25.704
They were taken at Lake Erie.

00:00:25.704 --> 00:00:28.074
- The thing that I find
amazes a lot of people

00:00:28.074 --> 00:00:31.743
that are familiar with them
is the sheer size of them.

00:00:31.743 --> 00:00:33.956
I've been with lots of people
that, for the first time

00:00:33.956 --> 00:00:34.927
when they're seeing them, they're like,

00:00:34.927 --> 00:00:36.706
"Really, this isn't an ocean?"

00:00:36.706 --> 00:00:38.983
- [Narrator] Dave Sandford is
a professional photographer

00:00:38.983 --> 00:00:41.315
from Ontario, Canada.

00:00:41.315 --> 00:00:43.977
To do his work, Sandford
waits for the perfect

00:00:43.977 --> 00:00:47.982
weather conditions, heading
out into fierce winds

00:00:47.982 --> 00:00:51.649
and ice cold water to
make his masterpieces.

00:00:54.542 --> 00:00:57.292
(waves crashing)

00:00:58.865 --> 00:01:01.448
(upbeat music)

00:01:07.777 --> 00:01:11.540
- Lake Erie is in the most
southern part of Ontario.

00:01:11.540 --> 00:01:13.950
It's the smallest of the Great Lakes.

00:01:13.950 --> 00:01:18.117
As far as the depth goes,
it's the shallowest by far.

00:01:19.181 --> 00:01:22.401
- Being a large lake,
but relatively shallow

00:01:22.401 --> 00:01:24.922
compared to the other Great Lakes,

00:01:24.922 --> 00:01:27.505
creates unique wave conditions.

00:01:28.561 --> 00:01:32.588
The waves on Lake Erie
tend to be closer together

00:01:32.588 --> 00:01:36.122
with a lot more of the violence
on the white water on top.

00:01:36.122 --> 00:01:37.440
- There's a lot of people that have

00:01:37.440 --> 00:01:38.903
lost their lives on Lake Erie.

00:01:38.903 --> 00:01:42.136
There's been estimated
somewhere over 8,000 shipwrecks

00:01:42.136 --> 00:01:43.807
on Lake Erie over the years.

00:01:43.807 --> 00:01:46.343
- At her worst, Lake
Erie can be murderous.

00:01:46.343 --> 00:01:50.510
In fact when she's murderous,
we call her a widow maker.

00:01:55.502 --> 00:01:57.903
- To be a nature
photographer, I think that one

00:01:57.903 --> 00:02:00.812
of the number one things you
have to have is patience.

00:02:00.812 --> 00:02:04.510
The first wave photo that
I posted from Lake Erie

00:02:04.510 --> 00:02:06.754
I came home that night
and I posted an image

00:02:06.754 --> 00:02:11.233
and right away the
number of comments on it,

00:02:11.233 --> 00:02:14.859
it was crazy compared to
what I normally would get,

00:02:14.859 --> 00:02:19.026
and the number of ‘likes.’ (laughs)

00:02:23.531 --> 00:02:25.621
If I know I'm going in the
water, it starts from like

00:02:25.621 --> 00:02:28.268
the moment I wake up in the morning.

00:02:28.268 --> 00:02:30.665
There's a lot of mental preparation.

00:02:30.665 --> 00:02:32.780
There is an adrenaline rush too, for sure.

00:02:32.780 --> 00:02:35.964
Like I've no idea how
things are going to unfold.

00:02:35.964 --> 00:02:37.753
It's before the sun's even coming up,

00:02:37.753 --> 00:02:41.625
and you can see the water in the distance

00:02:41.625 --> 00:02:45.209
and those waves exploding into the air,

00:02:45.209 --> 00:02:47.792
and it's just such a cool rush.

00:02:48.991 --> 00:02:52.693
The window of opportunity to
photograph the lakes for me

00:02:52.693 --> 00:02:56.860
is generally from maybe
mid-October through early December.

00:02:58.066 --> 00:03:00.064
It's the best time of the
year when you still have

00:03:00.064 --> 00:03:03.084
that warm air masses that
are around this region

00:03:03.084 --> 00:03:06.088
and then those cold air masses
that are coming from up north

00:03:06.088 --> 00:03:09.755
that give you these
really prime conditions.

00:03:13.253 --> 00:03:17.286
Obviously weather changes
and it's constantly changing.

00:03:17.286 --> 00:03:20.462
The biggest factor is wind
speed and wind direction.

00:03:20.462 --> 00:03:24.651
A southwest wind is the
best, and minimal wind speeds

00:03:24.651 --> 00:03:28.078
I'm usually looking at
around 30 miles an hour

00:03:28.078 --> 00:03:29.581
at the bare minimum.

00:03:29.581 --> 00:03:32.283
At the high end of it,
you're actually getting into

00:03:32.283 --> 00:03:36.798
category one hurricane level
winds over 70 miles an hour.

00:03:36.798 --> 00:03:41.599
It's a crazy mass of water
and that's when you're getting

00:03:41.599 --> 00:03:44.932
these waves that are upwards of 30 feet.

00:03:48.823 --> 00:03:51.633
The tough thing about
lake waves is it's not

00:03:51.633 --> 00:03:54.813
like ocean waves where
you have sets that come in

00:03:54.813 --> 00:03:56.371
and then there's a bit of a lull.

00:03:56.371 --> 00:03:58.897
Lake waves are constantly pounding.

00:03:58.897 --> 00:04:01.009
You know you virtually
have like two seconds,

00:04:01.009 --> 00:04:03.182
2 1/2 seconds, between each wave,

00:04:03.182 --> 00:04:07.349
and where I'm at, it's more
like a giant washing machine.

00:04:08.275 --> 00:04:09.699
It's not easy.

00:04:09.699 --> 00:04:11.861
You're kinda cold after a while too.

00:04:11.861 --> 00:04:14.685
That water is only around
the 50 degree mark.

00:04:14.685 --> 00:04:16.845
There's days where
there's ice in the water

00:04:16.845 --> 00:04:20.851
and you're literally you're
like, what am I doing? (laughs)

00:04:20.851 --> 00:04:22.724
Water is very powerful,
it's very deceiving,

00:04:22.724 --> 00:04:25.253
and you have to have great respect for it.

00:04:25.253 --> 00:04:28.388
You can get pulled under and
pulled out with a rip current.

00:04:28.388 --> 00:04:30.989
It's obviously a lot
stronger than you are.

00:04:30.989 --> 00:04:35.156
You have to know your limitations
and know when to say when.

00:04:38.759 --> 00:04:41.197
The waves that are generated
from this refraction

00:04:41.197 --> 00:04:45.962
off the pier, those are the
waves that I'm photographing

00:04:45.962 --> 00:04:49.016
where you have two masses
of water, two waves,

00:04:49.016 --> 00:04:52.029
that are moving in opposite
directions that are meeting

00:04:52.029 --> 00:04:55.696
and colliding and they actually,
they’ll hit and they twist

00:04:56.654 --> 00:05:00.737
and they turn and it's a
phenomenal thing to see.

00:05:01.591 --> 00:05:04.604
It's just building this
massive liquid mountain

00:05:04.604 --> 00:05:08.771
that literally lasts all of
like a second, if that, you know.

00:05:09.744 --> 00:05:13.244
Sometimes it's a fraction
of a moment in time,

00:05:13.244 --> 00:05:15.363
and that's one of the things
that I love is the challenge

00:05:15.363 --> 00:05:17.993
of capturing that moment.

00:05:17.993 --> 00:05:20.098
- [Narrator] Dave Sandford's
Liquid Mountain Photographs

00:05:20.098 --> 00:05:23.313
have appeared in several
online publications

00:05:23.313 --> 00:05:26.449
including The Washington Post.

00:05:26.449 --> 00:05:29.575
He also sells prints on his website.

00:05:29.575 --> 00:05:31.727
- I guess one of the things
that I really hope is

00:05:31.727 --> 00:05:35.477
that my photos can allow
people to appreciate

00:05:37.675 --> 00:05:41.842
what they have as far as nature
goes and learn to love it

00:05:43.291 --> 00:05:47.117
and cherish it and
embrace it and protect it.

00:05:47.117 --> 00:05:49.867
(dramatic music)

